@*
    Find — everything the phone has been told, read back.

    THE HALF OF MEMORY NOBODY COULD REACH. LearnAsync has run on every utterance
    for a long time: typed input through DeviceConversation.HeardAsync, spoken
    turns through MemoryEffects.OnTurnEnded. RecallAsync had exactly one caller
    in the whole repo, and it was in the native sample that is being retired. So
    the phone accumulated everything anybody said to it and had no screen that
    could tell them any of it back.

    IRemembers DIRECTLY, NOT CircleAISession.SearchAsync.

    The native screen called the static SearchAsync, which merges memory with
    kept transcripts by reading CircleAISession.Remembers and .Transcripts. Both
    of those statics are deliberately NULL in this head - setting Remembers here
    would add a third writer to the same store and record every turn twice, which
    DeviceConversation says in as many words: "calling it here as well would
    record every spoken sentence twice."

    So this page asks the injected IRemembers, which IS the store the app writes
    to, and does not go near the session.

    TRANSCRIPTS ARE NOT SEARCHED HERE, AND THAT IS AN HONEST GAP RATHER THAN A
    DESIGN. This head never registers IKeepsTranscripts at all: Transcribe
    transcribes a recording and throws the text away when you leave the screen.
    The native head kept them. Until this head keeps them there is nothing to
    search, and a second empty source would only make the result look thin for a
    reason nobody could see.
*@
@page "/find"
@inject IRemembers Remembers

<div class="find">

    <label class="find-label" for="find-q">What are you looking for?</label>

    <div class="find-row">
        <input id="find-q" class="find-input" type="search" @bind="_query"
               @bind:event="oninput" @onkeydown="OnKey"
               placeholder="Anything you have told it"
               disabled="@_busy" />
        <button class="find-go" @onclick="Search" disabled="@(_busy || Blank)">
            @(_busy ? "Looking…" : "Find")
        </button>
    </div>

    <p class="find-note">
        Looks through what you have told it. Everything stays on this phone.
    </p>

    @if (_status is { Length: > 0 })
    {
        <p class="find-status" role="status">@_status</p>
    }

    @if (_hits.Count > 0)
    {
        <ul class="find-hits">
            @foreach (var hit in _hits)
            {
                <li class="find-hit">
                    <span class="find-hit-text">@hit.Text</span>
                    @*
                        HOW SURE, NOT WHEN. Remembered carries Text and Confidence
                        and no timestamp, so a date here would be invented. The
                        confidence is real and it is the thing that separates
                        something the person stated outright from something the
                        extractor inferred.
                    *@
                    @if (hit.Confidence < 0.95)
                    {
                        <span class="find-hit-sure">about @(hit.Confidence * 100)% sure</span>
                    }
                </li>
            }
        </ul>
    }

</div>

@code {
    string _query = "";
    string _status = "";
    bool _busy;

    readonly List<Hit> _hits = [];

    bool Blank => string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(_query);

    /// <summary>One thing found, flattened so the markup needs no store types.</summary>
    sealed record Hit(string Text, double Confidence);

    async Task OnKey(KeyboardEventArgs e)
    {
        if (e.Key is "Enter" or "NumpadEnter") await Search();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Ask the store, and say plainly when it has nothing.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// AN EMPTY RESULT IS NOT AN ERROR AND MUST NOT READ LIKE ONE. A phone that
    /// has been talked to twice has almost nothing to recall, and "nothing
    /// matched" on a blank screen reads as broken. The line says what the store
    /// is FOR, so an empty answer teaches rather than alarms.
    /// </remarks>
    async Task Search()
    {
        if (_busy || Blank) return;

        _busy = true;
        _status = "";
        _hits.Clear();

        try
        {
            var found = await Remembers.RecallAsync(_query.Trim(), 20);

            foreach (var r in found)
                _hits.Add(new Hit(r.Text, r.Confidence));

            _status = _hits.Count == 0
                ? "Nothing matched yet. Things you tell it show up here."
                : $"{_hits.Count} found";
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            // The reason, not a generic failure - a store that cannot be opened
            // and a store with nothing in it are different problems.
            _status = Trouble.Say(ex);
        }
        finally
        {
            _busy = false;
        }
    }

}
